  "helpGroupsTagsTitle1": "Groups & tags",
  "@helpGroupsTagsTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsIntro": "Groups and tags are two different ways to sort the same address book. A contact belongs to groups you build by hand, and carries tags you type as short labels. Both are yours — the app never creates one on its own.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsTitle2": "Groups",
  "@helpGroupsTagsTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet1": "Open the Contacts tab and tap the group icon in the top bar to see all your groups.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet2": "Create a group, give it a name, and add members. A contact can be in more than one group.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet3": "A group can carry its own ringtone. Pick one from the phone's ringtones or from an audio file in your folders.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet4": "The group ringtone is used for members who do not have their own ringtone set. A ringtone on the contact always wins.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsTitle3": "Tags",
  "@helpGroupsTagsTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet5": "A tag is a short word you attach to a contact while editing them — \"plumber\", \"school\", \"trek group\". There is no fixed list; type whatever fits.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet6": "The Tags tab at the bottom of the app shows every tag in use as a cloud. A tag used by more contacts is drawn larger.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet7": "Tap a tag to see everyone who carries it. From there you can call, message, or open any of them.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet8": "Tags also work as an exception list for quiet hours, so a whole tag can be allowed to ring through.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsTitle4": "Working on many contacts at once",
  "@helpGroupsTagsTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet9": "Long-press a contact in the list to start selecting. Tap more contacts to add them to the selection.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet10": "The top bar then offers \"Select all\" and \"Delete selected\", so you can clear out many contacts in one step.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsBullet11": "Tap the cross in the top bar to leave selection mode without changing anything.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpGroupsTagsFooter": "Tip: use a group when the set is fixed and you want one ringtone for it. Use a tag when you only want to find those people again later.",
  "@helpGroupsTagsFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockTitle1": "App lock & PIN",
  "@helpAppLockTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockIntro": "App lock puts a screen in front of the whole app when you open it. You pick how it is unlocked under Settings → Security → App lock. There are three choices.",
  "@helpAppLockIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockTitle2": "The three modes",
  "@helpAppLockTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet1": "Off — the app opens straight away. Secret contacts still ask for an unlock separately.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet2": "Device lock — uses your phone's own fingerprint, face, or screen-lock PIN. This choice is greyed out until you set a screen lock in Android settings.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet3": "App PIN — a separate PIN just for this app, typed on a keypad inside the app. Useful when other people know your phone PIN.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockTitle3": "Setting up an App PIN",
  "@helpAppLockTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet4": "Choose a PIN of 4 to 6 digits and confirm it.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet5": "You are then shown a one-time recovery code. Write it down or copy it somewhere safe — it is shown once and never again.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet6": "The PIN is not stored as you typed it, and nobody can read it back out of the app.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockTitle4": "If you forget the App PIN",
  "@helpAppLockTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet7": "Tap \"Forgot PIN?\" on the lock screen and enter your recovery code.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet8": "A correct code switches App lock off and lets you in. Set a new PIN afterwards if you still want the lock.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet9": "Without the recovery code there is no way past the lock. That is deliberate — a back door for you would be a back door for anyone.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockTitle5": "When you are asked again",
  "@helpAppLockTitle5": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet10": "The lock screen returns when you come back to the app after leaving it, not on every screen inside it.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockBullet11": "The back gesture cannot dismiss it. Only a correct unlock, or the recovery code, lets you through.",
  "@helpAppLockBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpAppLockFooter": "App lock guards the door. Your secret contacts, backups, and sync have their own unlock on top of it — see the Biometric lock guide.",
  "@helpAppLockFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsTitle1": "Contact tools",
  "@helpContactToolsTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsIntro": "Three smaller tools that are easy to miss: contacts that delete themselves, the messaging apps a contact can be reached on, and the search index that makes the dialer find people fast.",
  "@helpContactToolsIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsTitle2": "Ephemeral (temporary) contacts",
  "@helpContactToolsTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet1": "While adding or editing a contact, switch on \"Ephemeral contact\". The entry then removes itself later, on its own.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet2": "Choose how long it lives: 2 hours, 24 hours, 7 days, or \"Auto-delete after 1 call\".",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet3": "Good for a delivery driver, a cab, or a one-off seller — the number is there when you need it and gone afterwards.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet4": "Opening the contact shows a banner counting down to its removal. From there you can add another 24 hours, or tap to keep it for good.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet5": "The app checks about once a minute, so a contact disappears shortly after its time is up rather than at the exact second.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsTitle3": "Connected apps",
  "@helpContactToolsTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet6": "If a messenger such as WhatsApp, Telegram or Arattai has synced itself against a contact on your phone, that contact shows a row of those apps.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet7": "Tap one to open the chat or call in that app directly. Nothing is sent by this app — it simply opens the other one.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet8": "The row is read from your phone's own address book, so it appears only for contacts linked to a device contact, and only while contacts permission is granted.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsTitle4": "Contact counts & search index",
  "@helpContactToolsTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet9": "Settings → Contacts → Contact counts & search index shows how many contacts are on the phone and how many are in the app — the quickest way to see whether a sync worked.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet10": "The search index is what makes T9 keypad search, transliterated search, and name search fast.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet11": "The screen tells you either \"Index healthy — all contacts are findable\" or how many contacts have stale search keys.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet12": "When some are stale, a Rebuild button appears. Tap it and the keys are rebuilt for the whole address book.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsBullet13": "Rebuild this if search stops finding a contact you know is saved, or after restoring an old backup.",
  "@helpContactToolsBullet13": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactToolsFooter": "Tip: an ephemeral contact is deleted for real when its time is up. If you may want the number later, tap \"Keep permanently\" on the banner before it goes.",
  "@helpContactToolsFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceTitle1": "Call context & notes",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceIntro": "The app reads your own call history to tell you a little about a person before you ring them, while they ring you, and after you hang up. All of it is worked out on this phone from data you already have.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceTitle2": "Before you call",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet1": "Open a contact and you see a short summary: when you last spoke, how long that call lasted, and what you noted about it.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet2": "If the contact has an address with a city, the summary also shows the local time there — useful before calling someone in another country.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet3": "When there is enough history, it suggests the time of day this person usually answers.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceTitle3": "\"Likely to answer now\"",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet4": "The dialer shows a short row of contacts above the keypad. You choose what fills it in Settings → Dialer top contacts: Most recent, Family & friends, or Likely to answer now.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet5": "\"Likely to answer now\" puts the people who usually pick up at this hour first. It is worked out from your own Recents — how often calls at this time of day were answered.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet6": "This only changes the order of the row. The app never dials on its own here — every call is still a tap you make.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceTitle4": "While the phone is ringing",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet7": "For a saved contact, the call screen can show their relationship, how long it has been since you last spoke, and a birthday or anniversary coming up.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet8": "A pending reminder you set for that person is shown too, so you remember why you meant to speak.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceTitle5": "After the call",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceTitle5": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet9": "When a call ends, a \"How did it go?\" sheet can appear. Note how the call went, write down what you discussed, and set a follow-up reminder.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet10": "You can dictate the note instead of typing it — tap the microphone and speak. Speech is turned into text on the phone.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet11": "Everything you save joins that contact's timeline, which is what the next pre-call summary reads.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceBullet12": "If you would rather not be asked, turn the sheet off under Settings → SIM & calling → Post-call options.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIntelligenceFooter": "Privacy: none of this leaves the phone. There is no lookup service behind it — the app only reads your own contacts, call log, and notes from its encrypted database.",
  "@helpCallerIntelligenceFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },

  "helpBiometricsText": "Biometric lock",
  "@helpBiometricsText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsIntro": "SreerajP Contacts Sphere can ask for your fingerprint or face before it shows or moves your most private data. It uses your phone's own lock — the app never sees or stores your fingerprint or face.",
  "@helpBiometricsIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsTitle1": "Where you are asked",
  "@helpBiometricsTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet1": "Viewing your secret contacts. These are hidden from the normal contact list until you unlock them.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet2": "Exporting secret contacts, so a private contact cannot be sent out of the app without your say-so.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet3": "Opening \"Sync to Another Device\", because a sync can include your secret contacts.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet4": "Opening \"Backup & Restore\", because a backup can include them too.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet5": "Opening the audit log, which holds a full before-and-after record of your contacts.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet6": "Accepting a contact someone sends you over Bluetooth.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet7": "Opening the app at all, if you set App lock to \"Device lock\" under Settings → Security.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsTitle2": "What counts as \"you\"",
  "@helpBiometricsTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet8": "Any fingerprint or face you have set up on the phone is accepted.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet9": "If you have not set up a fingerprint or face, the phone falls back to your screen-lock PIN, pattern, or password.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet10": "App lock can instead use an App PIN, which is separate from the phone's lock. That one is checked by the app itself — see the \"App lock & PIN\" guide.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsTitle3": "Your privacy",
  "@helpBiometricsTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet11": "The check is handled by Android, not by SreerajP Contacts Sphere. The app only learns whether the unlock passed or failed.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsBullet12": "This works offline. Nothing about your fingerprint or face ever leaves the phone.",
  "@helpBiometricsBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBiometricsFooter": "Tip: set up a screen lock (fingerprint, face, or PIN) in Android settings. With no lock at all the check cannot run, so sync and backup warn you and then let you decide whether to go on.",
  "@helpBiometricsFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupText": "Backup & Restore",
  "@helpBackupText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupIntro": "A backup saves everything in the app into one file that you keep. You can use it to move to a new phone or to recover after a reset — even if the new app was installed from a different source.",
  "@helpBackupIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupTitle1": "What the backup holds",
  "@helpBackupTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet1": "All contacts and their details, call history, groups, relationships, and blocked / spam numbers.",
  "@helpBackupBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet2": "Contact photos and calling-card images are included inside the file.",
  "@helpBackupBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet3": "Your app settings, such as theme and accent color.",
  "@helpBackupBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet4": "Your emergency info card, with its emergency contacts and the \"show on lock screen\" switches.",
  "@helpBackupBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet5": "Custom ringtones are not included — they point at files on this phone that would not exist elsewhere.",
  "@helpBackupBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupTitle2": "Your password is the key",
  "@helpBackupTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet6": "The backup file is locked with a password you choose. The app does not store it anywhere.",
  "@helpBackupBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet7": "You need the same password to restore — on this phone or any other. Keep it somewhere safe.",
  "@helpBackupBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet8": "If you lose the password, the file cannot be opened. There is no way to recover it — that is what keeps your data private.",
  "@helpBackupBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupTitle3": "Restoring replaces everything",
  "@helpBackupTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet9": "Restoring DELETES what is currently in the app and rebuilds it as an exact copy of the backup.",
  "@helpBackupBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet10": "It is not a merge. If you want to combine two phones without losing data, use \"Sync to Another Device\" instead.",
  "@helpBackupBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupBullet11": "Restore a backup made with the same app version. A backup from a very different version may be refused.",
  "@helpBackupBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpBackupFooter": "Tip: after making a backup, the app opens the share sheet so you can save the file to Files, Drive, or send it to yourself. Store it somewhere other than this phone.",
  "@helpBackupFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingText": "T9 Dialing & Malayalam",
  "@helpT9DialingText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingIntro": "SreerajP Contacts Sphere features a smart multi-script T9 dialpad. You can search your contacts seamlessly using English or regional script key presses (Malayalam, Devanagari, etc.).",
  "@helpT9DialingIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingTitle1": "Malayalam Vowels Mapping (അ to അഃ)",
  "@helpT9DialingTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet1": "Key 2 (ക-ങ): Vowels അ, ആ + Matras ാ, ി, ീ",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet2": "Key 3 (ച-ഞ): Vowels ഉ, ഊ, ഋ + Matras ു, ൂ, ൃ",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet3": "Key 4 (ട-ണ): Vowels എ, ഏ, ഐ + Matras െ, േ, ൈ",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet4": "Key 5 (ത-ന): Vowels ഒ, ഓ, ഔ + Matras ൊ, ോ, ൌ, ൗ",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet5": "Key 9 (ള-റ): Anusvaram & Visargam (ം, ഃ) + Chillu letters (ൺ, ൻ, ർ, ൽ, ൾ, ൿ)",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingTitle2": "Why Vowels Aren't Printed on Key Labels",
  "@helpT9DialingTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet6": "Key legends display consonant group ranges (e.g. ക-ങ, ച-ഞ) to keep the dialpad clean and easy to read.",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet7": "Even though vowels are not printed on the button face, all vowels (അ-ഔ), matras, and chillu letters are fully mapped and active in T9 search.",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingTitle3": "Manglish & Transliteration Search",
  "@helpT9DialingTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet8": "English T9 key presses automatically match Malayalam names. For example, typing 2-6-4-5 (A-N-I-L) will match both \"Anil\" and \"അനിൽ\".",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingBullet9": "To change the script shown on the keys, use the \"Dialpad script\" card on the main Settings page.",
  "@helpT9DialingBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpT9DialingFooter": "Tip: the \"Dialpad script\" card offers Auto, Malayalam, Devanagari, Cyrillic, Arabic, Greek, or None. Auto follows the app language. Whichever you pick, search still matches every script — the setting only changes what is printed on the keys.",
  "@helpT9DialingFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncText": "Cloud Sync & Backup",
  "@helpCloudSyncText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncIntro": "SreerajP Contacts Sphere connects with Google, Microsoft, and CardDAV/WebDAV servers. You can use a single provider or decouple them — syncing live contacts with one service while backing up your encrypted database to another.",
  "@helpCloudSyncIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncTitle1": "Contact Sync vs Cloud Backup",
  "@helpCloudSyncTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncBullet1": "Online Contact Sync (Live 2-Way): synchronizes individual contact cards (names, phone numbers, emails) directly with Google People API, Microsoft Graph Contacts, or CardDAV address books. Synced contacts appear in your online address book.",
  "@helpCloudSyncBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncBullet2": "Encrypted Cloud Backup: exports a full, password-encrypted .csbak file containing your complete database (all contacts, call history, call notes, tags, settings, and emergency info) to cloud file storage (Google Drive AppData, Microsoft OneDrive, or WebDAV).",
  "@helpCloudSyncBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncTitle2": "Mixing cloud providers",
  "@helpCloudSyncTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncBullet3": "You can use Google for live contact sync while storing encrypted cloud backups on Microsoft OneDrive or a self-hosted WebDAV server.",
  "@helpCloudSyncBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncBullet4": "In Settings → Online Provider Sync, toggle \"Contact Sync: On\" and \"Cloud Backup: Off\" for your Google account.",
  "@helpCloudSyncBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncBullet5": "Add your Microsoft or WebDAV account separately and select it when uploading encrypted cloud backups in Settings → Encrypted Cloud Backup.",
  "@helpCloudSyncBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncTitle3": "Privacy & Security",
  "@helpCloudSyncTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncBullet6": "Secret Vault Contacts: contacts saved as Secret in SreerajP Contacts Sphere are app-only and are NEVER uploaded or synced to online contact providers (Google Contacts, Outlook, or CardDAV).",
  "@helpCloudSyncBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncBullet7": "Encrypted Payload: Cloud backup files (.csbak) are encrypted locally using PBKDF2 and AES-GCM with your personal passphrase before upload. The cloud provider cannot read your backup data.",
  "@helpCloudSyncBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCloudSyncFooter": "Tip: You can manage all configured accounts under Settings → Online Provider Sync. Each account can have independent toggles for live contact sync and cloud backup.",
  "@helpCloudSyncFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },

  "helpPersonalizationTitle1": "Look, sound & region",
  "@helpPersonalizationTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationIntro": "Almost everything about how the app looks, what it sounds like, and how it reads phone numbers can be changed. Here is where each setting lives.",
  "@helpPersonalizationIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationTitle2": "Theme and colour",
  "@helpPersonalizationTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet1": "Settings → Appearance → Theme Mode: Light, Dark, or System. System follows your phone's own dark-mode setting.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet2": "Settings → Appearance → Accent Color: pick a preset or build your own colour. The preview updates as you choose.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationTitle3": "Font and text size",
  "@helpPersonalizationTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet3": "Settings → Appearance → Typography & Text Size sets the font and how large text is drawn.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet4": "Three Malayalam-capable fonts are built in — Manjari, Anek Malayalam, and Noto Sans Malayalam. Each covers Malayalam and English, so names in either script stay readable.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet5": "The fonts ship inside the app. Nothing is downloaded, and this works with no internet.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationTitle4": "How the contact list reads",
  "@helpPersonalizationTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet6": "Settings → Contacts → Display & formatting sets the sort order (by first name or last name).",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet7": "The same screen can hide contacts that have no phone number, which tidies a list imported from an email account.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationTitle5": "Ringtones, volume and vibration",
  "@helpPersonalizationTitle5": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet8": "Settings → Ringtone → Volume & vibration sets the ringtone volume and whether incoming calls vibrate.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet9": "Settings → Ringtone → Per-SIM ringtones gives SIM 1 and SIM 2 different tones, so you know which line is ringing.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet10": "A group can carry its own ringtone, and any contact can be given one while editing them.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet11": "When several apply, the most specific wins: the contact's own tone first, then their group's, then the SIM's.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet12": "Ringtones are not included in backups or in a sync to another phone, because they point at a sound file on this phone.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationTitle6": "Default country",
  "@helpPersonalizationTitle6": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet13": "Settings → Default country tells the app which country your plain, un-prefixed numbers belong to.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet13": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet14": "It is what lets the app see that a call from +91 98765 43210 is the same person as the 98765 43210 saved in your contacts.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet14": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet15": "Blocked numbers are matched the same way, so a number blocked in local form still blocks the international form.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet15": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationBullet16": "Getting this wrong is the usual reason a saved contact shows up as an unknown caller.",
  "@helpPersonalizationBullet16": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPersonalizationFooter": "Tip: theme, accent colour, fonts and the default country travel to another phone on a sync. Ringtones and SIM choices stay behind, because they belong to this handset.",
  "@helpPersonalizationFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamTitle1": "Caller ID & spam filter",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamIntro": "When a number that is not in your contacts calls, the app tries to say something useful about it, and can make a suspected spam call ring quietly instead of loudly. Both are switches you control, and both work entirely on this phone.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamTitle2": "Caller identification",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet1": "Turn it on under Settings → SIM & calling → Identification → \"Caller identification\".",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet2": "An unknown caller gets a label built from what can be worked out locally: the telemarketing and service number series, numbers you yourself marked as spam, and the network's own verified-caller flag when it sends one.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet3": "The badge appears on the call screen — red for suspected spam, a softer colour for a telemarketing or service number.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet4": "No number is ever looked up on the internet. There is no caller ID database behind this and nothing is uploaded.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamTitle3": "Filter suspected spam",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet5": "The second switch on the same screen, \"Filter suspected spam\", makes flagged callers ring silently instead of loudly.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet6": "The call still comes through and still lands in Recents. You are simply not disturbed by it.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet7": "Use this when you want to see who called but not be interrupted. Use blocking when you do not want the call at all.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamTitle4": "Block unknown",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet8": "Settings → Contacts → Blocked numbers has a \"Block unknown\" switch for calls that arrive with no number or a hidden one.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet9": "With it on, those calls are rejected before your phone rings, and are still written into Recents as blocked so you can see that they happened.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet10": "It does not affect a number you simply have not saved — only calls with no caller number at all.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamTitle5": "Marking a number as spam",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamTitle5": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet11": "Long-press a call in Recents and choose \"Mark as spam\". The same action reads \"Not spam\" afterwards, so you can take the mark off again.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet12": "A spam mark is separate from blocking. The number can still ring you — it is now labelled, and the spam filter can silence it if that switch is on.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamBullet13": "Your own marks feed the caller identification label, so the next call from that number is recognised.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamBullet13": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallerIdSpamFooter": "Tip: identification and spam filtering both need the app to be your default phone app, because Android only lets the default dialer inspect a call before it rings.",
  "@helpCallerIdSpamFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportTitle1": "Import & export files",
  "@helpImportExportTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportIntro": "You can move contacts in and out of the app as ordinary files — a spreadsheet-friendly CSV, or a vCard (.vcf) that any phone or computer address book understands.",
  "@helpImportExportIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportTitle2": "Where to find it",
  "@helpImportExportTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet1": "Open the Contacts tab, tap the three-dot menu in the top bar, and choose \"Import / Export\".",
  "@helpImportExportBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet2": "Four choices appear: Import CSV, Export CSV, Import vCard (.vcf), and Export vCard (.vcf).",
  "@helpImportExportBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportTitle3": "Importing",
  "@helpImportExportTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet3": "You pick the file yourself through the system file picker. The app never browses your storage on its own.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet4": "Imported contacts are added to the app. When the import finishes you are told how many came in.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet5": "If the file brings in people you already have, run Contacts → menu → \"Find Duplicates\" afterwards to tidy up.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportTitle4": "Exporting",
  "@helpImportExportTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet6": "An export writes a file and then opens the system share sheet, so you decide where it goes.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet7": "An export file is plain and not password-protected. Treat it like a copy of your address book and delete it when you are done.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet8": "Secret contacts are left out of a normal export unless you turn on \"Include secret contacts in export\" under Settings → Contacts → Secret contacts & export.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet9": "That same screen has \"Export secret contacts\", which saves a separate file holding only the secret ones. It asks for your fingerprint, face, or PIN first.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet10": "For a full, password-locked copy of everything — call history, photos, settings and all — use Settings → Backup & Restore instead.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportTitle5": "AirQR: sending more than one QR code can hold",
  "@helpImportExportTitle5": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet11": "A single QR code cannot hold a photo or a long contact card. AirQR splits the data across many frames and plays them as an animated QR code.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet12": "Open a contact, choose \"Share as QR code\", then tap the Air-Gap Stream button in that dialog to start the animation.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet13": "On the other phone, open Contacts → menu → \"Scan QR code\" and point the camera at the animation. It shows the progress while the frames come in and saves the contact once they are all there.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet13": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportBullet14": "Nothing is sent over Bluetooth, Wi-Fi or the internet — the only path is the camera looking at the screen. Keep both phones steady until it completes.",
  "@helpImportExportBullet14": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpImportExportFooter": "Tip: vCard (.vcf) is the safer choice for moving to another phone, because it keeps multiple numbers, emails and photos. CSV is best when you want to open the list in a spreadsheet.",
  "@helpImportExportFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityText": "Privacy, Security & Vault",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityIntro": "SreerajP Contacts Sphere is built from the ground up to guarantee uncompromising privacy, encrypted local storage, and granular security controls.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityTitle1": "Secret Contacts Vault",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet1": "What is a Secret Contact? Any contact marked as \"Secret\" is completely hidden from the main contact list, T9 dialer searches, and general export files.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet2": "Seeing them: tap the padlock icon in the top bar of the Contacts tab and unlock with your fingerprint, face, or device PIN. The list then shows the secret contacts alongside the rest.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet3": "Tap the padlock again to hide them. They also hide when you leave the contact list, so they are never left showing behind you.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityTitle2": "Biometrics & App PIN Protection",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet4": "You can secure the entire app or sensitive sections using your device's biometric sensors (fingerprint / face unlock).",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet5": "If your phone has no biometric hardware, or you want a code separate from your phone PIN, set an App PIN under Settings → Security → App lock. See the \"App lock & PIN\" guide.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityTitle3": "Screenshot Guard",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet6": "Screenshot guard blocks screenshots, screen recording, and the preview Android shows in Recents, while you are on a screen holding private data.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet7": "Turn it on or off under Settings → Security → Screenshot guard.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityTitle4": "Security Audit Log",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet8": "The audit log records every change to a contact — created, edited, or deleted — with what it looked like before and after.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet9": "Open an entry to see exactly what changed, and undo it if the change was a mistake.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet10": "Entries are chained together with a cryptographic hash, so an entry cannot be quietly altered or removed without it showing.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityBullet11": "Settings → Security → Audit log, which asks for your unlock first. It can also export a signed copy of the log.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPrivacySecurityFooter": "Security principle: everything is stored on this phone in a database encrypted with a key held in the phone's hardware keystore. There is no tracking, no advertising, and no server of ours to talk to.",
  "@helpPrivacySecurityFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },

  "helpContactSharingText": "Sharing & Card Scanning",
  "@helpContactSharingText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingIntro": "Quickly exchange contact information using modern digital QR codes, on-device business card OCR camera scanning, and offline Bluetooth LE.",
  "@helpContactSharingIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingTitle1": "QR Code Sharing & Scanner",
  "@helpContactSharingTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet1": "Show a QR code: open a contact, tap Share, and choose \"Share as QR code\". A standard vCard QR appears on screen for someone else to scan.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet2": "Scan a QR code: open the Contacts tab, tap the three-dot menu, and choose \"Scan QR code\" to open the camera.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet3": "What was scanned is shown to you first. You save it as a new contact only after looking at it.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingTitle2": "Business Card Scanner (On-Device AI)",
  "@helpContactSharingTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet4": "Photograph any paper business card with your phone's camera.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet5": "ContactSphere's optical character recognition (OCR) scans the image in seconds to extract names, phone numbers, emails, addresses, and company titles.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet6": "You can review, edit, or untick any field before saving to your address book.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet7": "100% On-Device Privacy: The card photo is processed locally on your phone and is never uploaded to any cloud server.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingTitle3": "Offline Bluetooth LE Share",
  "@helpContactSharingTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet8": "Share contacts directly with nearby Android devices running ContactSphere without internet or pairing codes.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet9": "The sender opens a contact, taps Share, and chooses \"Share via Bluetooth\". The receiver opens the Contacts tab, taps the three-dot menu, and chooses \"Bluetooth transfer\".",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet10": "The receiving phone shows a challenge you must confirm, so a contact cannot be pushed onto your phone without you agreeing.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingBullet11": "Devices automatically discover each other and transfer the contact securely over low-energy radio.",
  "@helpContactSharingBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSharingFooter": "Tip: every sharing method here uses the standard vCard format, which Android, iOS and desktop address books all understand. To send a whole address book as a file instead, see the Import & export guide.",
  "@helpContactSharingFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeText": "Duplicate Contacts & Merge",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeIntro": "Keep your address book clean and clutter-free with ContactSphere's intelligent duplicate detection and safe one-tap merging system.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeTitle1": "How Duplicates are Detected",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet1": "Same phone number: two contacts share the same digits, or the same number once it is put into full international form.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet2": "Same name: two contacts have the same full name, or the same name once it is transliterated — so \"Anil\" and \"അനിൽ\" are seen as one person.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet3": "Matching spreads across a set: if A matches B and B matches C, all three are shown together as one set.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet4": "Email addresses are deliberately not used, and neither are sound-alike name codes. Both produced wrong merges between unrelated people.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeTitle2": "Smart Merging Process",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet5": "Open the Contacts tab, tap the three-dot menu, and choose \"Find Duplicates\".",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet6": "Each set is shown as one card. The contact that will be kept is at the top; the others are ticked to be merged into it.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet7": "Untick anyone who does not belong in the set, or tap a different row to keep that one instead.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet8": "All the different phone numbers, emails, addresses, birthdays and notes from the set are carried over into the contact you keep. Nothing is thrown away.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet9": "Merge one set with its own Merge button, or use \"Merge all sets\" at the bottom to do the whole list at once.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeTitle3": "Safety & Reversibility",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet10": "Before merging everything at once, make a backup under Settings → Backup & Restore. A merge cannot be undone from the duplicates screen.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeBullet11": "If a merge was wrong, open the kept contact and edit it — the extra numbers and details are all still there, so you can move them back out into a new contact.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpDuplicateMergeFooter": "Tip: run \"Find Duplicates\" after a phonebook sync or a file import — that is when duplicates usually appear.",
  "@helpDuplicateMergeFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncText": "Contact Sync",
  "@helpContactSyncText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncIntro": "Sync keeps the app and your phone in step. You control each direction yourself — nothing here runs automatically. Open it from Settings → Contacts → Device & cloud sync.",
  "@helpContactSyncIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncTitle1": "The two normal actions",
  "@helpContactSyncTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet1": "Add device contacts to app: copies the phone's address book into the app. It only adds or updates — it never deletes.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet2": "Add app contacts to device: copies your app contacts into the phone. It only adds or updates — it never deletes. Your \"Me\" contact and secret contacts are never sent to the phone.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncTitle2": "Destructive sync",
  "@helpContactSyncTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet3": "The two \"(destructive)\" actions make the target an exact copy of the source. As well as adding and updating, they delete extras — so use them with care. Each one asks you to confirm first.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet4": "Add device contacts to app (destructive): after importing, it deletes app contacts that came from the phone but are no longer on it. It never deletes your \"Me\" contact, your secret contacts, or any contact you created only in the app.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet5": "Add app contacts to device (destructive): after copying, it deletes device contacts that are not in the app. Device contacts that match your \"Me\" contact or a secret contact are never deleted, even though those are never copied to the phone.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncTitle3": "Call log",
  "@helpContactSyncTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet6": "The phone's call log syncs into Recents on its own — when the app starts, when you open Recents, and when a call ends. Calls made from another dialer, or while the app was closed, come in this way. You do not have to do anything.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet7": "Add device call log to app: brings in the phone's older call history in one go, further back than the automatic sync reaches. It skips calls the app already has, so running it again is safe.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet8": "Add device call log to app (destructive): clears Recents and rebuilds it from the phone's call log. Any call notes or feedback you saved in the app are lost.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncBullet9": "There is no \"app to device\" for the call log: Android owns the phone's call log and records calls on its own.",
  "@helpContactSyncBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpContactSyncFooter": "Tip: a destructive sync cannot be undone. If you are unsure, make a backup first (Settings → Backup & Restore).",
  "@helpContactSyncFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningText": "Call Screening & Blocking",
  "@helpCallScreeningText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningIntro": "The app can turn a call away before your phone rings. Blocking is a list you build yourself, checked on this phone against the incoming number — nothing is looked up anywhere else.",
  "@helpCallScreeningIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningTitle1": "How call screening works",
  "@helpCallScreeningTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet1": "When a call arrives, Android hands the number to the app's call screening service before the phone rings.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet2": "If the number is on your blocked list, the call is rejected straight away — no ring, no vibration, no incoming screen.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet3": "Numbers are matched after being put into full international form using your Default country, so a number blocked as 98765 43210 also blocks +91 98765 43210.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet4": "A blocked call is still written into Recents with a \"Blocked\" mark, so you can see who tried.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningTitle2": "Blocking a number",
  "@helpCallScreeningTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet5": "From Recents: long-press the call and choose \"Block number\". The same action then reads \"Unblock number\".",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet6": "During a call: tap Block on the call screen. This works while it is ringing and while you are talking.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet7": "By hand: Settings → Contacts → Blocked numbers, then add the number yourself.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet8": "Blocking a number that is on a call right now hangs that call up immediately, wherever you blocked it from.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningTitle3": "Callers with no number",
  "@helpCallScreeningTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet9": "Settings → Contacts → Blocked numbers also has a \"Block unknown\" switch, for calls that arrive with a hidden or withheld number.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet10": "Those calls are rejected before ringing and still recorded in Recents as blocked.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet11": "It does not affect ordinary numbers you have not saved — only calls that carry no number at all.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningTitle4": "Silencing instead of blocking",
  "@helpCallScreeningTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet12": "If you would rather see the call but not be disturbed, use \"Filter suspected spam\" under Settings → SIM & calling → Identification. Flagged callers then ring silently.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet13": "See the \"Caller ID & spam filter\" guide for how a caller gets flagged.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet13": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningTitle5": "Default phone app is required",
  "@helpCallScreeningTitle5": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet14": "Android only lets the default phone app inspect a call before it rings. Without that role, blocking cannot happen early enough.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet14": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningBullet15": "Settings → Permissions shows whether the app already holds the role, and lets you ask for it.",
  "@helpCallScreeningBullet15": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpCallScreeningFooter": "Privacy note: screening happens entirely on this phone, against your own list. No phone number is ever sent to a server, and there is no shared spam database behind it.",
  "@helpCallScreeningFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },

  "helpPermissionsTitle1": "Permissions explained",
  "@helpPermissionsTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsIntro": "Settings → Permissions lists everything the app can access, with a live status beside each one. This page explains what each is for, and what stops working if you say no.",
  "@helpPermissionsIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsTitle2": "Two kinds of permission",
  "@helpPermissionsTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet1": "Explicit — Android asks you, and you can say no or change your mind later. These are the ones with Granted / Denied beside them.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet2": "Implicit — declared when the app is built and granted by the system at install. There is no prompt, because they cannot reach your personal data on their own.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsTitle3": "For calling",
  "@helpPermissionsTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet3": "Default phone app — makes this the system dialer, so it can show its own in-call screen, full-screen incoming alerts, and screen calls before they ring. Without it, calling works but blocking and screening do not.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet4": "Phone & Call Log — place, answer and manage calls, and read back a call's real duration for Recents.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet5": "Foreground Call Service & Ringing — keeps a call alive and lets the app ring and show the incoming-call screen.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet6": "Screen off near ear — blanks the screen while the phone is against your ear so your cheek does not press buttons.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsTitle4": "For your contacts",
  "@helpPermissionsTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet7": "Contacts — read and sync your phone's address book. Without it, the app keeps its own contacts but cannot see or update the phone's.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet8": "Photos & Media — pick a profile photo from your gallery.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet9": "Camera — take a contact photo, scan a QR code, or scan a paper business card.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet10": "Microphone — dictate a call note instead of typing it.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet11": "Location — tag a contact with a place, and needed by Android for Bluetooth scanning on older versions.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsTitle5": "For reminders and the emergency card",
  "@helpPermissionsTitle5": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet12": "Notifications — show reminders for birthdays, follow-ups and missed calls, and carry the emergency info card on your lock screen.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet13": "Alarms & reminders — lets Smart Redial call back at the time you set even when the app is closed.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet13": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet14": "Start after restart — puts your emergency info card back on the lock screen after the phone reboots.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet14": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsTitle6": "For sharing and sync",
  "@helpPermissionsTitle6": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet15": "Bluetooth Scan, Connect and Advertise — find a nearby phone, connect to it, and be findable while sharing a contact over Bluetooth.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet15": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet16": "Internet & Wi-Fi — used only to copy your data to another phone across your own local Wi-Fi during device sync. No cloud server is contacted unless you set up online sync or cloud backup yourself.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet16": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet17": "Biometrics — unlock secret contacts, and confirm before you export or sync data that may include them.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet17": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsTitle7": "Saying no, and changing your mind",
  "@helpPermissionsTitle7": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet18": "Every permission is asked for only when you first use the feature that needs it. Nothing is requested at install.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet18": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet19": "Refusing one disables just that feature. The rest of the app keeps working.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet19": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsBullet20": "A permission you denied twice shows as \"Blocked\". Android will not ask again — use the settings button in the top bar of the Permissions screen to change it by hand.",
  "@helpPermissionsBullet20": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpPermissionsFooter": "The app has no advertising or analytics code and contacts no server of ours. Anything that leaves the phone leaves because you set up a sync, a share, or a cloud backup.",
  "@helpPermissionsFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoText": "Emergency info",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoText": {
    "description": "Help: text in an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoIntro": "The emergency card holds a few facts that could help someone who finds you unwell — your blood group, your allergies, and who to call. It can be read on your lock screen without your PIN.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoIntro": {
    "description": "Help: lead paragraph of an article. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoTitle1": "What \"without unlocking\" means",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoTitle1": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet1": "While the card is on, a notification called \"Emergency info\" sits on your lock screen. Tapping it opens the card straight away — no PIN, fingerprint, or face needed.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet1": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet2": "The phone stays locked. Only the card opens; the rest of the app, and everything else on the phone, stays shut.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet2": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet3": "Android keeps its own \"Emergency information\" page behind the lock screen Emergency button. That page belongs to the phone maker, and no app can write into it — which is why SreerajP Contacts Sphere uses its own notification instead.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet3": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoTitle2": "You choose every line",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoTitle2": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet4": "The whole feature is off until you switch it on.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet4": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet5": "Each field has its own \"Show on lock screen\" switch. A field you leave switched off never leaves the app.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet5": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet6": "The preview at the bottom of the edit screen shows exactly what a stranger would see.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet6": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet7": "Switching the card off removes the notification and wipes the copy the lock screen was reading. What you typed stays saved inside the app.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet7": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoTitle3": "Calling for help",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoTitle3": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet8": "Each person you add gets a Call button on the card. Tapping it dials them right away from the lock screen.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet8": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet9": "People picked from your contacts are copied onto the card as a name and one number. Editing that contact later does not change the card — open this screen and save again.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet9": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoTitle4": "If the card is missing from the lock screen",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoTitle4": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet10": "Your phone decides which notifications the lock screen shows. Open Settings → Notifications → Notifications on lock screen and pick \"Show conversations, default and silent\".",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet10": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet11": "If that is set to \"Hide silent notifications\" or \"Don't show any notifications\", the card cannot appear there. No app can override that choice.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet11": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet12": "Also check that notifications for SreerajP Contacts Sphere are on, and that the \"Emergency info\" notification is not turned down to silent. The edit screen warns you when either is the case, and the button there opens the right settings page.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet12": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet13": "The card stays in the notification shade all the time on purpose — it is meant to be one tap away, and it cannot be swiped off by accident.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet13": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoTitle5": "How it is stored",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoTitle5": {
    "description": "Help: title (article title, topic card title or section heading). Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet14": "Your full record stays in the app's encrypted database, like the rest of your contacts.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet14": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet15": "Only the lines you switched on are copied into a small, plain file that the lock-screen card can read while the phone is locked. That copy cannot be encrypted — a locked phone has no way to unlock it for a stranger.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet15": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet16": "The copy stays inside the app's private storage. Other apps cannot read it, and it is left out of phone backups.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet16": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet17": "The card is saved inside a password-protected SreerajP Contacts Sphere backup, so a restore on a new phone brings it back.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet17": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoBullet18": "It also travels on a Full Sync to another phone, or when you tick \"Emergency info card\" while choosing what to share. The other phone only takes it if it has no card of its own — your card never replaces someone else's.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoBullet18": {
    "description": "Help: one bullet point in an article section. Help prose."
  },
  "helpEmergencyInfoFooter": "Tip: keep it short. Blood group, serious allergies, and one or two people to call are worth far more to a helper than a long medical history.",
  "@helpEmergencyInfoFooter": {
    "description": "Help: closing tip of an article. Help prose."
  },

